<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  robotiq_2f_85_module.urdf.xacro

  The Robotiq 2F-85 as a module of a work cell. It wraps the vanilla
  robotiq_description macros (UR-to-Robotiq adapter plus gripper; never edit
  that package, see third_party/vendored.yaml) and adds what a cell needs on
  top of them

  1. The module's borders (border.urdf.xacro), instance_mount on parent and
     the TCP instance_tcp on it, with the offset from
     modules/robotiq_2f_85.yaml. The module defines the TCP so that swapping
     the gripper swaps the TCP with it.

  2. With contact_pads true, simulation-only contact pads on the fingertips.
     Gazebo's bullet-featherstone engine, the one the gripper's mimic joints
     need, turns the concave fingertip collision mesh into its convex hull,
     which reaches into the gap between the fingers. With it, a gripper
     lowered over a 65 mm block hits the block and pushes it away, and
     closing fingers tip it before the pads touch. So each fingertip gets a
     pad of three boxes following the mesh, a link of its own on a fixed
     joint, its inner face on the flat pad face of the mesh so the grasp
     width is unchanged, and the fingertip mesh is switched off for
     collisions.

     Both halves are forced by the engine. Collision filter masks act per
     link in bullet-featherstone, not per collision, so the pad must not be
     lumped into the fingertip link (preserveFixedJoint), and the mask 0 on
     the fingertip then turns off its mesh and nothing else. Replacing the
     mesh itself is not possible from outside the package, and Gazebo
     resolves package URIs through the ROS package index, so a mesh overlay
     on GZ_SIM_RESOURCE_PATH is not consulted either.

     With the pads on, the pads are also the only parts of the fingers that
     collide in Gazebo. The upper finger links (knuckle with the lumped finger
     link, inner knuckle) collide there far wider than their meshes, in a way
     not reproduced by the meshes' convex hulls (with a 46 mm cell they stopped
     the fingers at 28 mm of travel, and neighbouring cells at 12 mm, where by
     the meshes nothing touches before the pads at 43 mm). Their collisions get
     collide_bitmask 0 as well. By the meshes these links reach a part only
     after the pads do, so nothing is lost; the palm keeps its collision.

     A planning description may turn contact_pads on as well (the gantry's
     MoveIt description does) and leave the springs off. MoveIt does not read
     the Gazebo extensions; the module descriptor's collision_replacements
     then switch the vanilla fingertip meshes off in the generated SRDF. Those
     meshes are convex hulls in the collision geometry, about twice the real
     fingertip, and overlapped neighbouring cells 60 mm apart.

  3. With sim_gazebo and include_ros2_control, the gripper's ros2_control
     block comes from this module, not from the vanilla macro (which is told
     include_ros2_control false). It is the vanilla block's Gazebo variant
     with one difference, the knuckle's command limits are off. Since
     Lyrical the controller manager clamps every command to the joint's
     limits, and for a position command that means within one control period
     at the URDF velocity limit of the current position, 0.005 rad ahead at
     0.5 rad per s and 100 Hz. The simulated knuckle is then driven only 5 mrad
     ahead of where it is, so the fingers creep and stop at the slightest
     resistance (the mimic linkage, a box nearby), long before the pads reach
     the part, and the driver reports a stall. Gazebo still holds the joint
     inside its URDF limits.

     With raise_finger_velocity_limits, for engines without mimic
     constraints (dartsim), the six finger joints get 10 rad per s as their
     velocity limit in Gazebo, set by a Python system
     (cell_description.gz_joint_velocity_limits), the URDF's stay as they
     are. There gz_ros2_control drives the knuckle and the five joints that
     mimic it with velocity commands (the knuckle at 50, the mimic joints at
     500 times their error per second), and dartsim drops a joint's velocity
     command while the joint is at its velocity limit, 0.5 rad per s in the
     vanilla URDF, or at a position limit. The joint then coasts. The
     knuckle ran on past its target until the pads met (0.33 rad commanded,
     0.77 reached), and the fingertips, which keep their orientation while
     the knuckle turns and so take every jump of its speed the other way,
     reached 0.5, coasted onto their position limit and stayed there, splayed
     out of the parallel linkage, until the knuckle moved again (Oct 2026).
     With 10 rad per s no joint gets near its limit; the gripper's speed comes
     from sim_gripper_driver, which ramps every command (knuckle_speed).
     bullet-featherstone has native mimic constraints and does not need it.

  4. With contact_compliance true, a simulation-only spring between the flange
     and the adapter, along the flange's z, for force control against
     dartsim's rigid contacts. The shared gripper_compliance macro
     (gripper_compliance.urdf.xacro, the reasoning is there), with the
     2F-85's mass. Planning descriptions leave it off.

  Parameters, the same names as robotiq_hande_module's
    prefix                 link name prefix
    parent                 the output border the module's mount hangs on
                           (tool0 on a UR, a changer's coupling)
    instance               the module's name in the cell, the borders' prefix
                           (gripper gives gripper_mount and gripper_tcp)
    name                   ros2_control system name of the gripper
    include_ros2_control   with sim_gazebo, the module's own block (see 3.),
                           otherwise passed to the gripper macro
    com_port               passed to the gripper macro (the real driver's
                           RS-485 port)
    sim_gazebo             passed to the gripper macro (GazeboSimSystem)
    contact_pads           the simulation-only fingertip pads described above
    contact_compliance     the simulation-only spring described in 4.
    pad_compliance         with contact_pads, rubber layers that yield (see the
                           pad macro); the Hand-E module has none yet
    drive_mimic_finger     with the module's own Gazebo block, list the five
                           mimic joints, which gz_ros2_control then drives
                           (default true; it does both under dartsim and
                           under bullet-featherstone)
    raise_finger_velocity_limits
                           in Gazebo, raise the finger joints' velocity limits
                           (see 3.), for dartsim

  Keep comments here free of colons (launch_ros reads robot_description as
  YAML first).
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
  <xacro:include filename="$(find robotiq_description)/urdf/ur_to_robotiq_adapter.urdf.xacro"/>
  <xacro:include filename="$(find cell_description)/urdf/gripper_compliance.urdf.xacro"/>
  <xacro:include filename="$(find cell_description)/urdf/border.urdf.xacro"/>
  <xacro:include filename="$(find robotiq_description)/urdf/robotiq_2f_85_macro.urdf.xacro"/>

  <!-- Two steps on purpose. The find substitution is only expanded in a plain
       attribute value, not inside an expression, so the path has to be
       resolved into a property before load_yaml is called on it. -->
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_2f_85_module_file" value="$(find cell_description)/modules/robotiq_2f_85.yaml"/>
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_2f_85_module_cfg" value="${xacro.load_yaml(robotiq_2f_85_module_file)}"/>

  <!-- One fingertip pad, see 2. above. Three boxes following the visual
       fingertip mesh in the fingertip link's frame (z along the tool axis
       towards the tip, the gripping face at 25.26 mm towards the other
       finger, which is the side inward). The gripping pad, 6.4 mm thick, the
       only part reaching below 12 mm. The web behind it, kept inside the
       mesh's slope. The upper body, full width. A single box over the whole
       mesh reached 25 mm further out at the tip than the real fingertip, so
       a closed gripper sat on the rim of a 47 mm hole instead of reaching its
       floor. The right fingertip mesh is the left one mirrored in x, offset y
       in y.

       Surfaces. The gripping face is the 2 mm rubber layer of the real
       fingertip, a link of its own (robotiq_85_<side>_finger_rubber_link)
       so it can carry its own friction, 0.9; everything else is the
       aluminium finger, 0.35. dartsim takes the lower coefficient of a
       contact, so on a cell (0.7, PVC sleeve) the rubber grips with 0.7 and
       the aluminium slides with 0.35, which also lets a finger that grazes a
       neighbouring cell slide past it. Friction goes into each collision by
       name (the per-link mu1 and mu2 never reached the collisions in the
       conversion), as ode mu and mu2 for dartsim and bullet friction for
       bullet-featherstone. Typical handbook values, not measured. -->
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_pad_rubber_mu" value="0.9"/>
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_pad_finger_mu" value="0.35"/>
  <xacro:macro name="robotiq_2f_85_friction" params="name mu">
    <collision name="${name}">
      <surface>
        <friction>
          <ode><mu>${mu}</mu><mu2>${mu}</mu2></ode>
          <bullet><friction>${mu}</friction><friction2>${mu}</friction2></bullet>
        </friction>
      </surface>
    </collision>
  </xacro:macro>
  <!-- With pad_compliance the rubber layer gives along the closing direction:
       a prismatic joint pushing it back into the finger, 10 kN/m (2 mm at
       20 N), damped near critical, travel the layer's 2 mm, never past the
       gripping face. dartsim has no deformable bodies in reach (its soft
       bodies are not loaded from SDF) and ignores contact stiffness in
       surface, so a joint spring is the only way to a fingertip that yields.
       The link weighs 20 g rather than a rubber layer's few grams: a smaller
       mass on this spring oscillates too fast for the 1 ms physics step. -->
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_pad_stiffness" value="10000.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_pad_damping" value="30.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_pad_travel" value="0.002"/>
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_pad_rubber_mass" value="0.02"/>
  <xacro:macro name="robotiq_2f_85_contact_pad" params="prefix side inward y compliant:=false">
    <link name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link">
      <inertial>
        <mass value="0.005"/>
        <inertia ixx="1e-6" iyy="1e-6" izz="1e-6" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyz="0"/>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Collisions unnamed on purpose, sdformat then calls them
           <link>_collision, _collision_1, _collision_2, the names the
           surface extensions below refer to.
           The gripping pad's aluminium part; the rubber layer is the next link. -->
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="${inward * 0.02108} 0 0.03151" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry>
          <box size="0.00436 0.0220 0.03902"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <!-- The web behind it. -->
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="${inward * 0.01245} 0 0.0170" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry>
          <box size="0.0129 0.0162 0.0100"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <!-- The upper body. -->
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="${inward * 0.00895} 0 0.0030" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry>
          <box size="0.0299 0.0270 0.0180"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>
    <joint name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_tip_link"/>
      <child link="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 ${y} 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_joint">
      <preserveFixedJoint>true</preserveFixedJoint>
    </gazebo>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link">
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_friction name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link_collision" mu="${robotiq_pad_finger_mu}"/>
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_friction name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link_collision_1" mu="${robotiq_pad_finger_mu}"/>
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_friction name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link_collision_2" mu="${robotiq_pad_finger_mu}"/>
    </gazebo>
    <link name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_link">
      <inertial>
        <mass value="${robotiq_pad_rubber_mass if compliant else 0.001}"/>
        <inertia ixx="1e-6" iyy="1e-6" izz="1e-6" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyz="0"/>
      </inertial>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="${inward * 0.02426} 0 0.03151" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <geometry>
          <box size="0.0020 0.0220 0.03902"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>
    <xacro:if value="${compliant}">
      <!-- Axis towards the other finger; compressed is negative. -->
      <joint name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_joint" type="prismatic">
        <parent link="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link"/>
        <child link="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <axis xyz="${inward} 0 0"/>
        <limit lower="${-robotiq_pad_travel}" upper="0.0" effort="1000.0" velocity="1.0"/>
        <dynamics damping="${robotiq_pad_damping}"/>
      </joint>
      <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_joint">
        <springStiffness>${robotiq_pad_stiffness}</springStiffness>
        <springReference>0.0</springReference>
      </gazebo>
    </xacro:if>
    <xacro:unless value="${compliant}">
      <joint name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_pad_link"/>
        <child link="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      </joint>
      <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_joint">
        <preserveFixedJoint>true</preserveFixedJoint>
      </gazebo>
    </xacro:unless>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_link">
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_friction name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_rubber_link_collision" mu="${robotiq_pad_rubber_mu}"/>
    </gazebo>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_tip_link">
      <collision name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_tip_link_collision">
        <surface>
          <contact>
            <collide_bitmask>0x00</collide_bitmask>
          </contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- The upper finger links of one side collide with nothing in Gazebo (see
       2. above). The finger link is lumped into the knuckle link in Gazebo;
       its extension still names the finger link and its own collision. -->
  <xacro:macro name="robotiq_2f_85_no_collision" params="prefix side">
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_knuckle_link">
      <collision name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_knuckle_link_collision">
        <surface><contact><collide_bitmask>0x00</collide_bitmask></contact></surface>
      </collision>
    </gazebo>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_link">
      <collision name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_finger_link_collision">
        <surface><contact><collide_bitmask>0x00</collide_bitmask></contact></surface>
      </collision>
    </gazebo>
    <gazebo reference="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_inner_knuckle_link">
      <collision name="${prefix}robotiq_85_${side}_inner_knuckle_link_collision">
        <surface><contact><collide_bitmask>0x00</collide_bitmask></contact></surface>
      </collision>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>

  <!-- See 3. above. The vanilla 2f_85.ros2_control.xacro's Gazebo variant,
       with the knuckle's command limits disabled. -->
  <!-- See 4. above. The mass on the spring (adapter, gripper, pads, with
       pad_compliance their 20 g rubber layers) for its rest offset. -->
  <xacro:property name="robotiq_2f_85_compliance_mass" value="1.029"/>

  <xacro:macro name="robotiq_2f_85_gazebo_ros2_control" params="name prefix compliance:=false pad_compliance:=false drive_mimic:=true">
    <ros2_control name="${name}" type="system">
      <hardware>
        <plugin>gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem</plugin>
      </hardware>
      <joint name="${prefix}robotiq_85_left_knuckle_joint">
        <limits enable="false"/>
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="position">
          <param name="initial_value">0.7929</param>
        </state_interface>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
      </joint>
      <xacro:if value="${drive_mimic}">
        <!-- Gazebo resolves these as mimic joints of the knuckle. Newer
             gz_ros2_control takes them from the URDF mimic tags; Humble's only
             drives a joint that names its mimic and multiplier here, the
             values of the vanilla macro's mimic tags. -->
        <xacro:macro name="robotiq_mimic_joint" params="joint multiplier">
          <joint name="${prefix}${joint}">
            <param name="mimic">${prefix}robotiq_85_left_knuckle_joint</param>
            <param name="multiplier">${multiplier}</param>
          </joint>
        </xacro:macro>
        <xacro:robotiq_mimic_joint joint="robotiq_85_right_knuckle_joint" multiplier="-1"/>
        <xacro:robotiq_mimic_joint joint="robotiq_85_left_inner_knuckle_joint" multiplier="1"/>
        <xacro:robotiq_mimic_joint joint="robotiq_85_right_inner_knuckle_joint" multiplier="-1"/>
        <xacro:robotiq_mimic_joint joint="robotiq_85_left_finger_tip_joint" multiplier="-1"/>
        <xacro:robotiq_mimic_joint joint="robotiq_85_right_finger_tip_joint" multiplier="1"/>
      </xacro:if>
      <xacro:if value="${compliance}">
        <!-- Passive, see 4. above. State only, so TF follows the spring. -->
        <xacro:gripper_compliance_state_joint prefix="${prefix}"/>
      </xacro:if>
      <xacro:if value="${pad_compliance}">
        <!-- The rubber layers' springs, passive like the wrist spring. -->
        <joint name="${prefix}robotiq_85_left_finger_rubber_joint">
          <state_interface name="position"/>
          <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        </joint>
        <joint name="${prefix}robotiq_85_right_finger_rubber_joint">
          <state_interface name="position"/>
          <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        </joint>
      </xacro:if>
    </ros2_control>
  </xacro:macro>

  <xacro:macro name="robotiq_2f_85_module"
               params="prefix:='' parent instance:=gripper name:=RobotiqGripper include_ros2_control:=false
                       com_port:=/dev/ttyUSB0 sim_gazebo:=false contact_pads:=false
                       contact_compliance:=false pad_compliance:=false
                       drive_mimic_finger:=true raise_finger_velocity_limits:=false
                       tcp_offset:='0 0 0'">
    <xacro:property name="frames" value="${robotiq_2f_85_module_cfg['frames']}"/>
    <xacro:property name="mount" value="${prefix}${instance}${frames['mount']}"/>
    <xacro:border name="${mount}" parent="${parent}"/>
    <!-- tcp_offset, three numbers, a cell's measured correction (gantry_description
         config tool_calibration.yaml): where the real fingers' centre is, seen from
         the module's TCP, in the TCP's frame (rpy 0, so the mount's axes). The whole
         gripper moves by it, the TCP with it: moving only the TCP left the
         fingers in MoveIt's model 3.4 mm beside a part the TCP was centred on, and
         every grip of a cell in the real box touched it or the box (2 Nov 2026). -->
    <xacro:property name="tcp_dxyz" value="${[float(v) for v in str(tcp_offset).split()]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="gripper_base" value="${mount}"/>
    <xacro:if value="${tcp_dxyz != [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]}">
      <xacro:property name="gripper_base" value="${prefix}${instance}_calibrated_link"/>
      <link name="${gripper_base}"/>
      <joint name="${gripper_base}_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="${mount}"/>
        <child link="${gripper_base}"/>
        <origin xyz="${tcp_dxyz[0]} ${tcp_dxyz[1]} ${tcp_dxyz[2]}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      </joint>
    </xacro:if>
    <xacro:if value="${contact_compliance}">
      <xacro:gripper_compliance prefix="${prefix}" parent="${gripper_base}" mass="${robotiq_2f_85_compliance_mass}"/>
      <xacro:ur_to_robotiq prefix="${prefix}" parent="${prefix}robotiq_compliance_link" child="${prefix}robotiq_adapter_top_link"/>
    </xacro:if>
    <xacro:unless value="${contact_compliance}">
      <xacro:ur_to_robotiq prefix="${prefix}" parent="${gripper_base}" child="${prefix}robotiq_adapter_top_link"/>
    </xacro:unless>

    <xacro:property name="own_ros2_control" value="${sim_gazebo and include_ros2_control}"/>
    <xacro:robotiq_gripper
        name="${name}"
        prefix="${prefix}"
        parent="${prefix}robotiq_adapter_top_link"
        sim_gazebo="${sim_gazebo}"
        include_ros2_control="${include_ros2_control and not own_ros2_control}"
        com_port="${com_port}">
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </xacro:robotiq_gripper>
    <xacro:if value="${own_ros2_control}">
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_gazebo_ros2_control name="${name}" prefix="${prefix}" compliance="${contact_compliance}"
                                               pad_compliance="${contact_pads and pad_compliance}"
                                               drive_mimic="${drive_mimic_finger}"/>
      <!-- See 3. above. -->
      <xacro:if value="${raise_finger_velocity_limits}">
        <gazebo>
          <plugin filename="gz-sim-python-system-loader-system" name="gz::sim::systems::PythonSystemLoader">
            <module_name>cell_description.gz_joint_velocity_limits</module_name>
            <joints>${prefix}robotiq_85_left_knuckle_joint ${prefix}robotiq_85_right_knuckle_joint ${prefix}robotiq_85_left_inner_knuckle_joint ${prefix}robotiq_85_right_inner_knuckle_joint ${prefix}robotiq_85_left_finger_tip_joint ${prefix}robotiq_85_right_finger_tip_joint</joints>
            <velocity>10.0</velocity>
          </plugin>
        </gazebo>
      </xacro:if>
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="tcp" value="${robotiq_2f_85_module_cfg['tcp']}"/>
    <xacro:property name="tcp_frame" value="${prefix}${instance}${frames['tcp']}"/>
    <link name="${tcp_frame}"/>
    <joint name="${tcp_frame}_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${gripper_base}"/>
      <child link="${tcp_frame}"/>
      <origin xyz="${tcp['xyz'][0]} ${tcp['xyz'][1]} ${tcp['xyz'][2]}"
              rpy="${tcp['rpy'][0]} ${tcp['rpy'][1]} ${tcp['rpy'][2]}"/>
    </joint>

    <xacro:if value="${contact_pads}">
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_contact_pad prefix="${prefix}" side="left" inward="-1" y="-0.0008" compliant="${pad_compliance}"/>
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_contact_pad prefix="${prefix}" side="right" inward="1" y="0.00005" compliant="${pad_compliance}"/>
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_no_collision prefix="${prefix}" side="left"/>
      <xacro:robotiq_2f_85_no_collision prefix="${prefix}" side="right"/>
    </xacro:if>

  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
